==English==

===Etymology===
{{der|en|la|-}} {{compound|la|floccus|t1=a wisp|naucum|t2=a trifle|nihilum|t3=nothing|pilus|t4=a hair|nocat=1}} + {{m|en|-fication}}

A jocular coinage, apparently by pupils at {{w|Eton College}},<ref>{{cite-journal|title=Mind your language |author=Dot Wordsworth |magazine={{w|The Spectator}}|archiveurl=https://web.archive.org/web/20200613003918/https://www.spectator.co.uk/article/mind-your-language-11-june-2011|location=London|publisher={{w|Press Holdings}}|date=11 June 2011|issn=0038-6952|oclc=1766325}}</ref> combining a number of Latin word stems. The word was inspired by a line present in various editions of [[w:William Lily (grammarian)|William Lily's]] (c. 1468–1522) Latin grammars published around the 17th–19th centuries (including the ''Eton Latin Grammar''),<ref>{{cite-book|en|author=R. R.|title=An Engliſh Grammar: Or, A plain Expoſition of Lilie's Grammar|location=London|publisher={{...|Printed by}} Felix Kyngston{{nb...}}|year=1641|page=107|pageurl=https://books.google.ca/books?id=2PZmAAAAcAAJ&pg=RA1-PA107}}</ref><ref>{{cite-book|en|title=Lilies Rules Construed,{{nb...|Whereunto are added Tho. Robinsons Heteroclites, The Latin Syntaxis, and Qui nihi.}}|location=London|publisher={{...|Printed by}} Roger Norton,{{nb...}}|year=1659|page=54|pageurl=https://books.google.ca/books?id=uosnrGdnIZsC&pg=PA54|column=1}}</ref><ref>{{cite-book|author=T. W. C. Edwards|chapter=Syntaxis, or the Rules of Grammar Construed|title=The Eton College Grammar: A Plain and Concise Introduction to the Latin Language, being Lily's Grammar Abridged,{{nb...|For the Use of the Young Gentlemen of Eton College: But with the Addition of Many Useful Notes and Observations, and Also of the Accents and Quantity.}}|location=London|publisher={{...|Printed for}} W. Simpkin and R. Marshall,{{nb...}}|year=1826|page=269|pageurl=https://archive.org/details/etonlatingramma02collgoog/page/n282/mode/2up}}</ref> in which some nouns commonly used in the [[genitive]] case with some verbs like {{m|la|pendo}} and {{m|la|facio}} are listed, which express evaluating something as worthless or as previously mentioned:

"{{l|la|flocci|Flócci}} ''of a lock of wool,'' {{l|la|nauci|náuci}} ''of a nut-shell'', {{l|la|nihili|níhili}} ''of nóthing'', {{l|la|pili|píli}} ''of a hair'', {{l|la|assis|ássis}} ''of a pénny'', {{l|la|huius|hújus}} ''of this'', {{l|la|teruncii|terúncii}} ''of a fárthing'', addúntur ''are ádded'', peculiáritèr ''pecúliarly or véry próperly'' vérbis ''to verbs'' æstimándi ''of esteéming''."

